---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { href } from "../lib/paths";

const agentPrompt = `You are setting up gojo for the Git repository that is your current working directory.

Goals:
1) Install gojo from source (if not already available)
2) Configure a local gojo instance
3) Register THIS repository as a gojo project, add a first agent, sync, run it, and confirm success

Do not push to a remote unless I explicitly ask. Prefer commit-only integration.

────────────────────────────────────────
1) Install gojo (sibling directory — not inside this repo)
────────────────────────────────────────
- Ensure Bun 1.2+ and Git are installed.
- If \`gojo\` / \`bun run gojo\` is not already available:
  - Clone https://github.com/detroitpro/gojo into a sibling directory, e.g. \`../gojo\`
  - In that clone: \`bun install\`
- Use that clone for all \`bun run gojo …\` commands below (or add it to PATH as you prefer).

────────────────────────────────────────
2) Configure a local instance (once)
────────────────────────────────────────
- If setup has not been done yet:
  \`bun run gojo setup --username <pick-a-username> --password '<strong-password>'\`
  (Choose credentials; do not invent and commit secrets.)
- Start the server if it is not running:
  \`bun run gojo server start\`
  (Foreground is fine for first setup; \`service install\` / \`service start\` is optional.)
- Run \`bun run gojo server doctor\` and fix obvious failures (Git, disk, adapters).
- Open http://127.0.0.1:7430 when the server is up.

────────────────────────────────────────
3) First agent for THIS repository: repo-brief
────────────────────────────────────────
Create these files in the current repository (the user's project, not the gojo clone).

Vocabulary reminder for \`gojo.yaml\`:
- \`profiles:\` — reusable adapter configuration (shell / cursor / claude-code, timeout, model).
- \`agents:\` — the work units. Each agent picks a profile and points at a promptFile.

A) \`gojo.yaml\` at the repo root:

\`\`\`yaml
version: 1

project:
  name: PROJECT_NAME
  defaultBranch: DEFAULT_BRANCH

repository:
  remote: origin
  syncBeforeRun: false
  requireCleanBase: false
  submodules: false
  gitLfs: false

profiles:
  shell:
    adapter: shell
    timeout: 5m

validationProfiles:
  repo-brief:
    steps:
      - name: file-exists
        command: test -f docs/REPO_BRIEF.md
        timeout: 30s
      - name: has-stack
        command: grep -q '^## Stack' docs/REPO_BRIEF.md
        timeout: 30s
      - name: has-layout
        command: grep -q '^## Layout' docs/REPO_BRIEF.md
        timeout: 30s
      - name: enough-content
        command: "wc -l docs/REPO_BRIEF.md | awk '$1 >= 20'"
        timeout: 30s

agents:
  repo-brief:
    description: Inventory the repo and write docs/REPO_BRIEF.md
    profile: shell
    promptFile: .gojo/agents/repo-brief.sh
    validationProfile: repo-brief
    integration:
      mode: commit-only
      targetBranch: DEFAULT_BRANCH
      requireAllValidations: true
      commitMessage: "gojo: add repository brief"

schedules: {}
notifications: {}
\`\`\`

Replace PROJECT_NAME with the folder basename and DEFAULT_BRANCH with the current git branch (e.g. main).

B) \`.gojo/agents/repo-brief.sh\`:

\`\`\`bash
#!/bin/sh
set -eu

mkdir -p docs

# Discover stack signals (best-effort; do not fail if missing).
STACK_NOTES=""
for f in package.json Cargo.toml go.mod pyproject.toml requirements.txt Makefile composer.json Gemfile; do
  if [ -f "$f" ]; then
    STACK_NOTES="$STACK_NOTES- Found \`$f\`\\n"
  fi
done
if [ -z "$STACK_NOTES" ]; then
  STACK_NOTES="- No common manifest files detected at repo root.\\n"
fi

LAYOUT="$(ls -1 | head -n 40 | sed 's/^/- /')"

README_HINT=""
if [ -f README.md ]; then
  README_HINT="$(head -n 40 README.md | sed 's/^/> /')"
else
  README_HINT="> No README.md at repo root."
fi

cat > docs/REPO_BRIEF.md <<EOF
# Repository brief

Generated by gojo agent \`repo-brief\` for operators and agents.

## Stack

$STACK_NOTES
Infer language/toolchain from the files above and any README signals.

## Layout

Top-level entries:

$LAYOUT

## How to work on it

$README_HINT

Summarize install / build / test / run steps when the README or manifests make them clear.
If unclear, say what is unknown.

## Open questions

- What is the primary command to run tests?
- What is the production deploy path (if any)?
- Are there required secrets or services for local development?
EOF

# Ensure the brief is substantive (padding if discovery was thin).
LINES="$(wc -l < docs/REPO_BRIEF.md | tr -d ' ')"
if [ "$LINES" -lt 20 ]; then
  {
    echo ""
    echo "### Notes"
    echo ""
    echo "- Expand this brief as you learn more about the repository."
    echo "- Keep section headings stable; agents and gojo validation depend on them."
    echo "- Prefer linking to existing docs over duplicating long guides."
  } >> docs/REPO_BRIEF.md
fi

mkdir -p .gojo
cat > .gojo/handoff.json <<'JSON'
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "runId": "01ARZ3NDEKTSV4RRFFQ69G5FAV",
  "status": "completed",
  "summary": "Wrote docs/REPO_BRIEF.md from repository discovery",
  "startingCommit": "unknown",
  "resultCommit": "unknown",
  "filesChanged": ["docs/REPO_BRIEF.md"],
  "validation": { "passed": true, "steps": [] },
  "decisions": [],
  "unresolvedIssues": [],
  "recommendedNextActions": [],
  "agentAssessment": { "successful": true, "confidence": 0.85 }
}
JSON
\`\`\`

Make the script executable (\`chmod +x .gojo/agents/repo-brief.sh\`).

Commit \`gojo.yaml\` and \`.gojo/agents/repo-brief.sh\` to the current repo (local commit is fine).

────────────────────────────────────────
4) Register, sync, run
────────────────────────────────────────
- ABS_PATH = absolute path of the current working directory
- BRANCH = current git branch
- NAME = basename of ABS_PATH

Commands (from the gojo clone, adjusting paths as needed):

\`\`\`bash
bun run gojo project add "$NAME" "$ABS_PATH" --branch "$BRANCH"
bun run gojo project list
bun run gojo project sync <project-id>
bun run gojo agent list --project <project-id>
bun run gojo agent run <repo-brief-agent-id>
bun run gojo run list
\`\`\`

Confirm the run reaches Succeeded, \`docs/REPO_BRIEF.md\` exists with \`## Stack\` and \`## Layout\`, and is at least ~20 lines.

If something fails, diagnose with \`gojo server doctor\`, \`gojo project doctor <id>\`, and run logs — then retry.

When done, report: gojo home path used, admin username chosen (not the password), project id, agent id, run id, and a short summary of REPO_BRIEF.md.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="gojo"
  description="Schedule, run, and audit autonomous coding agents against your Git repositories — with validation and merge under your control."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <img
      class="hero-media"
      src={href("/images/ui-dashboard.png")}
      alt=""
      width="1440"
      height="900"
      decoding="async"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="hero-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <p class="hero-brand">gojo</p>
      <h1>Run coding agents on a schedule you can trust.</h1>
      <p class="hero-lead">
        Self-hosted orchestration for Cursor, Claude Code, and shell agents — with isolated Git
        worktrees, real validation, and merges the platform owns.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={href("/getting-started")}>Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/first-agent")}>Your first agent</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/advanced-agent")}>Advanced agent</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Built for unattended agent work</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      gojo is the operations layer around your agents: schedule the work, isolate the repo, prove
      the result, then integrate it — without letting an adapter push straight to main.
    </p>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Schedule with intent</h3>
        <p>
          Cron and one-off runs, explicit time zones, overlap policies, retries, and automatic
          disable after repeated failures.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Isolated Git worktrees</h3>
        <p>
          Every attempt gets its own branch and worktree so concurrent agents never write over each
          other.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Adapters you already use</h3>
        <p>
          Shell for deterministic scripts; Cursor Agent and Claude Code for coding agents — detected
          from your machine and versioned per run.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Platform-owned success</h3>
        <p>
          Agents propose work. gojo runs your validation pipeline and decides whether the run
          actually succeeded.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="product">
    <div class="section">
      <h2>The ops console</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        A local web UI for projects, schedules, live runs, and audit — same control surface as the
        CLI, bound to localhost by default.
      </p>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src={href("/images/ui-dashboard.png")}
            alt="gojo dashboard showing project, agent, schedule, and recent run counts"
            width="1440"
            height="900"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Dashboard — scheduler ops overview and recent runs</figcaption>
        </figure>
      <div class="shot-grid">
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src={href("/images/ui-schedules.png")}
            alt="gojo schedules view with future-runs timeline and human-readable cron"
            width="1440"
            height="900"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Schedules — future-runs timeline and next-fire labels</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="shot">
          <img
            src={href("/images/ui-run-detail.png")}
            alt="gojo run detail with phase timeline, cost, and activity feed"
            width="1440"
            height="900"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Run detail — phase timeline, usage, and activity</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="ask-your-agent">
    <div class="section">
      <h2>Ask your agent to install gojo</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        Paste this into Cursor, Claude Code, or your coding agent while you're in the repository you
        want to schedule. It installs gojo, configures a local instance, and runs a first
        <span class="mono">repo-brief</span> agent against that repo.
      </p>

      <div class="agent-prompt-block">
        <div class="agent-prompt-toolbar">
          <span class="agent-prompt-label">Agent prompt</span>
          <button class="btn btn-primary btn-sm" type="button" id="copy-agent-prompt" data-copied="Copied">
            Copy prompt
          </button>
        </div>
        <pre class="agent-prompt" id="agent-prompt-text">{agentPrompt}</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="hero-cta hero-cta-follow">
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/getting-started")}>Getting started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/first-agent")}>Your first agent</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="section">
      <h2>From prompt to pull request</h2>
      <p class="section-lead">
        Define agents in a repo manifest or the UI. gojo prepares the workspace, invokes the
        adapter, validates, commits, and integrates according to policy.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Validation pipelines</h3>
          <p>Install, lint, test, and build steps you define — outside the adapter conversation.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Merge queue</h3>
          <p>
            Commit-only, pull request, await approval, or auto-merge — serialized per project so
            two green runs can’t race into the same branch.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Handoff reports</h3>
          <p>
            Structured summaries for the next run or human: what changed, what failed, what to do
            next.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature">
          <h3>Full run history</h3>
          <p>Live logs, diffs, artifacts, and an audit trail you can inspect from CLI or web UI.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Operate locally, stay in control</h2>
    <p class="section-lead">
      One binary (or Bun from source), SQLite on disk, and a web UI that binds to localhost by
      default.
    </p>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>CLI + web UI</h3>
        <p>
          Install as a systemd or launchd service. Manage projects, runs, and schedules from the
          terminal or the browser.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Notifications</h3>
        <p>Slack and generic webhooks when runs finish, fail, or schedules disable themselves.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Crash-safe scheduling</h3>
        <p>
          Leases prevent double-firing. Interrupted runs are recovered instead of silently marked
          successful.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature">
        <h3>Secrets & backups</h3>
        <p>
          Encrypted local secret store, scoped access, and backup/restore for config, database, and
          keys.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-cta hero-cta-follow">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href={href("/getting-started")}>Install gojo</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={href("/docs")}>Documentation</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    const button = document.getElementById("copy-agent-prompt");
    const promptEl = document.getElementById("agent-prompt-text");
    if (button && promptEl) {
      const original = button.textContent ?? "Copy prompt";
      const copiedLabel = button.getAttribute("data-copied") ?? "Copied";
      button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
        const text = promptEl.textContent ?? "";
        try {
          await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
          button.textContent = copiedLabel;
          window.setTimeout(() => {
            button.textContent = original;
          }, 1600);
        } catch {
          button.textContent = "Copy failed";
          window.setTimeout(() => {
            button.textContent = original;
          }, 1600);
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
